{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-146169",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =16,\\, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & <16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.1) \\\\  & =3.2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.75) \\\\  & =24 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.75) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-146169\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>Given a population in which a proportion <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"98\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45D TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"99\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-texatom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mover><mjx-over style=\"padding-bottom: 0.105em; padding-left: 0.335em; margin-bottom: -0.551em;\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-sop\" style=\"width: 0px; margin-left: -0.278em;\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C6 TEX-S1\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-over><mjx-base><mjx-mrow><mjx-mstyle><mjx-mspace style=\"width: 0.167em;\"></mjx-mspace></mjx-mstyle><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45D TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-mrow></mjx-base></mjx-mover></mjx-texatom></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mover><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> can be approximated as</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" display=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"100\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math display=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"margin-